A||||6||Frank Sombers||V-6 exhaust system on a 4 Cylinder? ||||5||
Z||000000||Frank Sombers||01-05-2001||02:17 PM||||From the Cat. back will a stock V-6 exhaust system fit on a 4 cylinder? I have an '88<BR>4 cylinder Cpe. and since the body is made for it, I would like to have tailpipes exiting from both sides of the car. I've read on other threads that the V-6 muffler and tailpipes will definitely work (a simple bolt on) but I haven't read if the V-6 system will match up to the Cat. I know Ocelot has a 4 cylinder dual exhaust system, but it isn't SS, and it seems that a lot of people are down on their products. My thoughts are that a stock SS V-6 system might be the better way to go. <BR>Also, if I go with a V-6 muffler and tailpipes is a high flow cat recommended or overkill? I'm concerned that with a V-6 system and a high flow cat. on a 4 banger I could develop some low end problems. <BR>Thanks for all of your time and opinions.<BR>Frank<P>  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||01-05-2001||03:38 PM||||Im wondering the same thing. Also do all 88 coupes have the cutout on the right side for the tale pipes? I know mine does.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||01-05-2001||05:06 PM||||it'll fit just fine.. as long as you have the rear facia provisions (cut outs) for the additional tips (i'm not familiar with the 4s rear bumper) as for low end torque issues you won't have any noticable or harmful decrease in tq. Aaron Billips (friend and Ga. Fieros Member) with aburgandy 87 4cyl has an ocelot for the 6 on his... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||David Gamache||01-05-2001||05:10 PM||||The 88 4 cyc fiero's all have the extra cut out for a dual exhaust because that was the same year, and the last year, the formula model was offered so rather than have 2 rear bumpers styles only the dual cut bumper was used. <BR>As for adding a dual exhaust to your single exhaust 4cyc the V-6 exhaust system will fit, but the pipe from the cat to the muffler is shaped slightly different than your stock piece and this section of pipe will rub the motor mount bracket near the frame.  This piece of pipe can be reshaped at a muffler shop or perhaps you could reuse the pipe from the 4cyc. Something else to keep in mind is that some of the early model SE 4cyc fiero's came with a dual exhaust, like the  V-6, and may be a direct fit replacement. I have refited a dual exhaust by reshaping the pipe I mentioned and currently have an SE 4cyc dual exhaust sitting in the garage and it definetly looks like it will go right into my next 88 fiero project. Hope that helps!    ||||reg||1
Z||000004||SloPonti||01-05-2001||05:13 PM||||yes it will work with any body style and any four banger! i replaced my single outlet 84 four bangers exhaust with a new v6 exhaust and i put a high flow cat on! bolts up just fine like it was suppose to be there!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/sloponti/fiero1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||David Gamache||01-05-2001||05:20 PM||||Almost forgot, The higher flow cat when added to my 88 fiero did not seem to produce any more power, but the NOX readings for smog testing were reduced quite a bit and the weight of the new cat was considerably less. There is also additional work space avaliable under the cat side of the engine near the starter now. One big drawback to many aftermarket cats is they use a filter "brick" and if the engine runs excessively hot, even for a short period of time, such as, when an egr goes out it will disolve this brick and all that is left is a hollow cat. If I had it to do over I would have kept the factory cat.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||01-05-2001||08:41 PM||||i have a stock gt exhaust with a free flow cat on my '87 4cyl. just cut out a spot on the passenger side. got tips like sloponti's. works good.||||reg||1
